    },
    "rich_text_nmTfUR": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_PqdJJL": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<strong>Challenge</strong>",
            "highlight_style": "none",
            "highlight_font_style": "italic",
            "highlight_style_color": "",
            "highlight_text_color": "",
            "heading_size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "text_UE4C6K": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>With so many new products, WLC faced the arduous task of transitioning from manual labeling to automation while accommodating the variety of labels required – each catering to different market segments and liquor types.</p><p>They needed a label partner who could deliver variety, quality, and cost-effectiveness.</p><p>The biggest obstacle: Each new product required a distinct design, fit, and material to complement its unique bottle shape and size. That meant a printer with the flexibility to offer a lot of changeovers and prototypes.</p><p>“Ultimately, when looking into different label printers, two things are crucial: quality and price,” Fabian Sacher, CEO of West Street Liquor Company, explained.</p><p>Usually, they would attend packaging trade shows to evaluate different vendors. But by chance, Sacher reached out to NADCO’s founders, Neil Doniger, and Rena’ Johnson, unaware that the couple were regular visitors to Aruba and could meet in person.</p>",
            "text_size": "text-base",
            "text_line_limit": "none",
            "button_style": "btn--underline",
            "button_icon": "none"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_PqdJJL",
